'''Federalism in Urcea''' describes both the federal governance system of Urcea, employing thirty-four [[Province (Urcea)|provinces]], [[Crownland (Urcea)|crownlands]], and [[State (Urcea)|states]], as well as attitudes and ideologies related to its use. Federalism, which finds its roots in many different areas including Urcea's feudal past and the concept of {{wp|Subsidiarity (Urcea)|subsidiarity}} among other sources, is a key part of the [[Constitution of Urcea]] and comprises a significant part of the political life of most Urceans. The thirty-four constituent entities of Urcea have significant latitude to pass laws, implement programs, and prosecute violators, playing an important role in shaping regionalized variations of Urcea's [[Economy of Urcea|economy]] and [[Society of Urcea|society]]. The provinces and other entities are also the primary organizing unit of many different institutions, including its [[Judiciary of Urcea|courts]], [[Local government in Urcea|local governments]], [[Guilds (Urcea)|guilds]], [[Political parties in Urcea|political parties]], and even the [[Urcean Conference of Catholic Bishops|Catholic Church hierarchy]] within the country. Views about Urcea's federal system shape Urceans conceptions of themselves (in terms of regionalized identity) but also form an important contrast between Urcea's main political parties and movements.
